Vintern-1B: An Efficient Multimodal Large Language Model for Vietnamese
Khang T. Doan1, Bao G. Huynh2 , Dung T. Hoang3, Thuc D. Pham4, Nhat H. Pham5, Quan T.M. Nguyen6, Bang Q. Vo7, Suong N. Hoang8
1dtkhangbk@gmail.com 2huynhgiabaoa2@gmail.com 3htdung167@gmail.com 4phamdinhthuc020100@gmail.com 5phamhuynhnhat99@gmail.com 6minhquan240600@gmail.com
7bavo.imp@gmail.com 8suong.nhoang@gmail.com
Abstract. In this report, we introduce Vintern-1B (Vietnamese-InternVL2-1B), a reliable 1-billion-parameters multimodal large language model (MLLM) for Vietnamese language tasks. By integrating the Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct [1] language model with the InternViT-300M-448px [2] visual model, Vintern-1B is optimized for a range of applications, including optical character recognition (OCR), document extraction, and general question-answering in Vietnamese context. The model is fine-tuned on an extensive dataset of over 3 million image-question-answer pairs, achieving robust performance and reliable results across multiple Vietnamese language benchmarks like OpenViVQA [3]. Vintern-1B is small enough to fit into various on-device applications easily. Additionally, we have open-sourced several Vietnamese vision question answering (VQA) datasets for text and diagrams, created with Gemini 1.5 Flash [4]. Our models are available at: https://huggingface.co/5CD-AI/ Vintern-1B-v2. Keywords: Vietnamese Multimodal Large Language Model, VQA, Qwen2, InternVL.
1. Introduction
The emergence of large language models (LLMs) has significantly transformed the field of natural language processing, demonstrating exceptional capabilities in tasks requiring complex reasoning and deep linguistic comprehension. Building on the success of LLMs, researchers have begun to explore multimodal large language models (MLLMs) [2] [4] [5] [6] [7] [8], which integrate visual data with textual information. These models have proven highly effective in tasks such as image captioning, visual question answering, and multimodal machine translation, where understanding the interaction between language and vision are crucial.
Despite global progress in developing MLLMs, the advancement of Vietnamese MLLMs has been hampered by the limited availability of high-quality multimodal datasets. While models like Qwen-VL [5], MiniCPM-Llama3-V-2.5 [9], MoE-LLaVA-Qwen [10], and InternVL [2] have shown promise in processing Vietnamese text, there remains a gap in resources and models fully supporting Vietnamese multimodal tasks.
In Vietnam, initiatives such as the V-Vistral [11] model, the Vista dataset [12], and the development of LaVy [13] have made significant contributions to the field. However, these models and resources currently lack support for Vietnamese OCR-related tasks and document processing, which are critical for a wide range of practical applications.
To address these limitations, we introduce Vintern-1B, an efficient 1-billion-parameter multimodal model specifically designed for Vietnamese language tasks. Vintern-1B based on InternVL2-1B [2], which integrates the Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct [1] language model with the InternViT-300M-448px [2] visual model, optimized for tasks such as OCR, document extraction, and general VQA. Additionally, we have created new OCR and document visual question

answering (DocVQA) datasets for the Vietnamese language, which have been used to fine-tune Vintern-1B. We provide a detailed description of Vintern-1B’s architecture, data creation, training procedures, and how to benchmark MLLMs using MLLM-as-a-Judge.
Through these contributions, we aim to advance the field of Vietnamese MLLMs, equipping researchers and practitioners with the tools and resources necessary to explore and innovate at the intersection of language and vision within the Vietnamese context.
2. Related work The exceptional performance of GPT-4V [8] extends GPT-4’s capabilities by incorpo-
rating visual inputs, allowing it to handle both text and image content, marking a significant advancement in the domain of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). Following this, Gemini Pro Vision [14] further enhances MLLMs by enabling the processing of text, images, and audio, while supporting up to 1 million tokens, significantly boosting performance.
Over the past year, many open-source MLLMs have gained prominence. Recent research has focused on developing MLLMs that achieve unified understanding and reasoning across different modalities, building on the success of LLMs. Models such as the LLaVA series, MiniGPT-4, Qwen-VL, Florence-2, and the InternVL series have demonstrated impressive results by aligning visual input with LLMs through visual instruction tuning.
In the context of Vietnamese language, research efforts such as V-Vistral [11] and LaVy [13] have made significant contributions to the progress of MLLMs. However, these models still exhibit notable gaps in understanding documents, charts, infographics, and recognizing scene texts within the Vietnamese context, compared to other open-source models in English or Chinese. This disparity is largely due to the lack of high-quality Vietnamese datasets for MLLMs.
In this report, we address this challenge by introducing Vintern-1B, an effective MLLM model for Vietnamese, fine-tuned on our large-scale, high-quality datasets. Additionally, we benchmark the model using MLLMs-as-judge, specifically designed to evaluate MLLMs’ understanding of Vietnamese visual language tasks.
3. Vintern-1B
3.1. Overall Architecture Vintern-1B model are built with InternVL-1.5 [2] architecture akin to widely-used open-
source MLLMs, specifically the “ViT-MLP-LLM” configuration referenced in various existing studies, see detailed in Figure 1. The architecture using three main components:
• Vision Encoder: InternViT-300M-448px [2] a distilled small vision foundation model, is used as the vision encoder.
• MLP Projector: A two-layer Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) projector is employed to align the output representations from the visual and language modalities. This projector ensures that the visual and textual information is transformed into a common space.
• Large Language Model: We utilize the pre-trained Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct [1], which is a language model responsible for generating textual information, take the aligned representations from the MLP projector
During training, the model uses module dynamic resolution strategy, dividing images into tiles of 448×448 pixels in sizes ranging from 1 to 12, based on the aspect ratio and resolution of the input images. During testing, this can be zero-shot scaled up to 40 tiles (i.e., 4K resolution). To enhance scalability for high resolution, a pixel shuffle operation is employed to reduce the number of visual tokens to one-quarter of the original. Therefore, in the model, a 448×448 image is represented by 256 visual tokens.

Figure 1. Overall Architecture. Vintern-1B adopts the ViT-MLP-LLM architecture similar to popular MLLMs ([2] [15] [16] [6]), combining a pre-trained InternViT-300M-448px [2] with Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct [1] through a MLP projector. The model also employ a simple pixel shuffle to reduce the number of visual tokens to one-quarter. Module Dynamic High Resolution dynamically match an optimal aspect ratio from pre-defined ratios, dividing the image into tiles of 448×448 pixels and creating a thumbnail for global context. This method minimizes aspect ratio distortion and accommodates varying resolutions during training.
3.2. Vietnamese VQA Dataset
With acknowledgment of the difference between Vietnamese images and IntervnVL’s images. To enhance the Vintern-1B model’s performance in the Vietnamese context, we extended several multimodal datasets. These datasets were designed to cover a wide range of tasks, from detailed image descriptions to OCR and document understanding, ensuring that the model is well-equipped to handle the nuances of Vietnamese visual and textual data.

3.2.1. Vietnamese Image Extension
We extended the Vietnamese multimodal datasets by collecting images from diverse sources, including web crawls across various topics relevant to Vietnamese culture and environment. These images include a wide array of content such as street scenes, traditional events, educational materials, and everyday objects, all of which reflect the unique characteristics of Vietnamese society. This extension is crucial for training the model to recognize and process visual information that is culturally specific to Vietnam. The datasets used in fine-tuning phase are summarized in Table 1.

